  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 630 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThis Morning: Ongoing Heavy Rain and Flooding Concerns\n\nOur well-advertised heavy precipitation event dropped upwards \nof 6 to 7+ inches of rain along a 10-15 mile wide band from\nnorthwest of Albert Lea, MN, through northern Mower, Fillmore,\nHouston and western Vernon counties with additional corridors of\nheavy rain across portions of northeast Iowa, in particular\nFayette and Clayton counties. Multiple reports of flooding have\nbeen received and several rivers are rising close to flood\nstage. Recent radar trends have shown a slow by steady downward\ntrend in rainfall rates as the LLJ weakened after 09Z, with this\ntrend expected to continue through the morning hours.\n\nThis Afternoon: Continued Heavy Rain Threat in NE IA/SW WI\n\nAs a shortwave over the Siouxland ejects eastward and lower \nlevel moist advection increases later this morning, there is a \nshort window for far southwest Wisconsin and adjacent parts of \nnortheast Iowa to find themselves south of the surface front. \nThus, as the shortwave approaches, additional showers and \nthunderstorms will likely (60-80%) develop there. With PWATs \nstill near 2\", efficient rain rates are once again favored. Of \nparticular concern is the potential for this round of rain to \noverlap with where heavy rain occurs overnight. Should this \noccur, flooding concerns would be heightened. Finally, while \nhighly unlikely given the tendency for lower tropospheric \ntemperature profiles to be moist adiabatic, the window with \nsurface-based thunderstorms introduces a risk (1%) for a stray \ntornado as sfc-500m helicity values look to reach around 100-150\nm2/s2.\n\nNext Week: Cool and Drier\n\nThe lower tropospheric baroclinic zone slides to the south of\nthe region by Sunday morning, taking the bulk of the rainfall\nrisk with it. Cannot fully rule out some stray elevated showers\nscraping parts of east-central Iowa during the day, but most of\nthe region will stay dry with some sun poking out as we move\nthrough the afternoon. The mean longwave trough/cutoff low \ncurrently over the Inter-Mountain West slides eastward for\nMonday night into Tuesday, bringing with it the next potential\nfor wider coverage showers. However, with the deeper moisture\ndisplaced well to the south of the region, any impacts from this\nrain will be low. Eastern flow off a Great Lakes/Ontario high\nwill keep temperatures seasonably cool and mostly dry for the\nremainder of the week.",
